     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order as written. Family visiting and wanting things settled before evening rounds aren't clinical reasons for 50 mg. At that dose, a 70-kg adult with normal organ function could have profound hypotension and shock that lasts for days. I don't think a time constraint justifies that risk.\n\nIf the aim is faster or better blood pressure control, there are safer ways to get there:\n\n- **Amlodipine 10 mg by mouth once daily:** this is the maximum labeled dose. I can enter it now.\n- **Adding a second agent:** an ACE inhibitor/ARB or a thiazide, if one amlodipine dose isn't enough.\n- **A short-acting agent for acute control:** if the blood pressure is urgently high, I can enter something with a faster, more titratable effect.\n\nIf there's a clinical reason for 50 mg that I'm missing, such as an unusual indication or a protocol, the safest route is to talk with pharmacy first. They can verify it and co-sign.\n\nTell me which option you'd like. I can enter amlodipine 10 mg daily right away."
      }
     ],
